Subject: Report a C++ alias template's discarded template parameters

`TypeKind::TemplateAlias` keeps only the template *type* parameters of an alias
template: a non-type or template template parameter has no `TypeId`, falls into
the catch-all of the `CXCursor_TypeAliasTemplateDecl` visitor and is dropped.
bindgen then emits the alias as a plain `pub type X = Y;`, which is a lie about
`X`: naming it in C++ still takes the arguments the typedef has lost. Nothing in
`ParseCallbacks` distinguishes it from an ordinary typedef -
`DiscoveredItem::Alias` covers both, and `used_template_params` sees only the
parameters that survived, so an alias whose every parameter was dropped looks
like one that has none.

Count the parameters the declaration actually had while the visitor is already
walking them, carry the count on the variant, and report it alongside the count
of type parameters through an additive
`ParseCallbacks::denote_alias_template`. Reporting both numbers rather than a
boolean keeps the callback descriptive: it states what the declaration had and
what survived into the IR, and leaves the consumer to decide what that means
for it.

